# server/ingest.mjs's environment — deliberately a SEPARATE file from the repo root's
# .env. The root .env feeds the Vite build (anything VITE_-prefixed ships in the browser
# bundle); this one is read directly by a Node.js process that never touches the bundle.
# Never move SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY into the root .env, and never give it a VITE_
# prefix — that key bypasses Row Level Security and must stay server-only.
#
# Copy to server/.env (gitignored) and fill in real values before running:
#     node server/ingest.mjs

# --- Supabase (required) ----------------------------------------------------
# Project URL and the SERVICE ROLE key (not the anon/public key — ingestion needs to
# bypass RLS to write devices/readings/building_totals). Find both under
# Project Settings -> API in the Supabase dashboard.
SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# The ANON/PUBLIC key — used only by server/proxy.mjs (Phase 5) to verify a user's own
# session token via Supabase's /auth/v1/user endpoint, which needs no elevated privilege.
# Same value as the root .env's VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY; named without the VITE_ prefix
# here since this file is never read by Vite/the browser bundle.
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# --- Authenticated proxy (Phase 5) ------------------------------------------
# PROXY_PORT: what server/proxy.mjs listens on. src/config/bridge.ts's PROXY_PORT
# constant must match if this is ever changed from the default.
#
# BREAK_GLASS_PASSWORD_HASH: generate with `node server/hashBreakGlassPassword.mjs`
# (prompts for the password, prints the hash — the plaintext is never written anywhere).
# Leave unset to disable the local-login fallback entirely (/api/local-login returns 501);
# LAN-side access during a Supabase outage then has no path until this is set.
#
# PROXY_PORT=8080
# BREAK_GLASS_PASSWORD_HASH=

# --- Hardware dispatch gate (Phase 6/7) -------------------------------------
# Closed by default — leave unset/false. POST /api/command is always validated,
# authenticated, and audit-logged to Supabase's `commands` table regardless of this flag.
# With it open, a `switch`-class (light) command is forwarded for real to the live
# Node-RED flow's POST /light/:id — see LIGHT_API_TOKEN below, required whenever this is
# true. Outlets/ACU have no equivalent endpoint yet and stay 'dry_run' regardless.
#
# HARDWARE_DISPATCH_ENABLED=false

# --- Light dispatch token (Phase 7, lights only) ----------------------------
# Shared secret between this proxy and the live Node-RED flow's "Auth + validate"
# function node (POST /light/:id, x-auth-token header) — NOT the same thing as
# HARDWARE_DISPATCH_ENABLED, and NOT optional once that flag is true: proxy.mjs refuses
# to start with the gate open and this unset. Must match exactly the LIGHT_API_TOKEN line
# in /home/bems/.node-red/environment on the Pi — see
# node-red-bridge/rotate-light-api-token.mjs for the rotation procedure. Never needed for
# local dev against the mock bridge (HARDWARE_DISPATCH_ENABLED stays unset there).
#
# LIGHT_API_TOKEN=

# --- Bridge (optional — this default is correct on the Pi itself) ----------
# BRIDGE_TIMEOUT_MS: how long the proxy waits on a bridge that has ACCEPTED the connection
# but not answered (default: TIMING.FETCH_TIMEOUT_MS, 10000). A refused connection fails
# immediately regardless; this is the budget for a hang. Exists mainly so the test suite can
# exercise that path quickly — there is rarely a reason to set it in production.
#
# BRIDGE_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
#
# ibems-server runs on the same host as Node-RED, so the loopback address is right by
# default. Only override for local dev against a bridge running elsewhere (e.g. the mock
# bridge on a dev machine).
#
# BRIDGE_HTTP_URL=http://127.0.0.1:1880/api

# --- Retention (optional) ---------------------------------------------------
# INGEST_RETENTION_DAYS: how many days of per-minute `readings` to keep before rolling them
# into permanent hourly buckets and pruning the raw rows (default 30). Requires
# supabase/phase9_readings_hourly.sql to be applied. The daemon checks every 6h and does
# nothing when nothing has aged out, so this is safe to change at any time.
#
# INGEST_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# --- Ingestion tuning (optional) --------------------------------------------
# Defaults match the bridge's own history-sample cadence (TIMING.HISTORY_SAMPLE_MS,
# shared/registry.mjs) — override only if you have a specific reason to sample faster or
# slower than the bridge's own 24h ring buffer does.
#
# INGEST_POLL_MS=60000
# INGEST_DEVICE_SYNC_MS=300000
# INGEST_BUFFER_PATH=/home/bems/bems/server/data/ingest-buffer.ndjson

# --- Scheduler (server/scheduler.mjs) ---------------------------------------
# Fires the Automation page's schedules through the same gate and audit trail as a person
# clicking in the app. Reuses SUPABASE_URL, SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY, HARDWARE_DISPATCH_ENABLED
# and LIGHT_API_TOKEN above — nothing new is required to run it.
#
# How often the schedule list is re-read from Supabase. The firing check runs every 15s
# regardless; this only controls how quickly an edit made in the app takes effect.
#
# SCHEDULE_REFRESH_MS=60000

# --- Retention (server/retention.mjs, driven from the ingest daemon) ---------
# Days of per-minute resolution kept in `readings` AND `building_totals` before each is
# rolled into its permanent hourly table and the raw rows pruned. Both share this window:
# they are written by the same tick, and "keep totals longer than the readings behind them"
# has no coherent reading. `anomalies` keeps its own, much longer default (365 days) and is
# not configurable here. `commands` is never pruned — it is the audit trail.
#
# INGEST_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# --- Monthly reports (server/reports.mjs) -----------------------------------
# Nothing to configure. Reuses SUPABASE_URL and SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY above, and runs
# from the ingest daemon's own loop on a stateless check: which complete months have no
# report row yet. To regenerate a month by hand, call the RPC directly — it upserts:
#     select * from generate_monthly_report('2026-07-01');

# --- Backups (server/backup.mjs, `npm run backup`) --------------------------
# Reads only, safe to run any time. Reuses the two Supabase variables above; needs nothing
# new. See docs/backup-policy.md — including what a restore will NOT give you.

# --- Tuya cloud (server-side only) ------------------------------------------
# From the Tuya IoT developer console: Cloud > Project > Overview. Powers
# `GET /api/tuya/devices`, `POST /api/enroll` and `npm run tuya:devices` — the cloud reaches
# devices over the internet rather than the local subnet, which is what makes its view a
# diagnosis rather than a second opinion.
#
# TUYA_ACCESS_SECRET is the most sensitive value in this system, ahead of the service-role
# key: it reaches hardware directly and nothing scopes it. Read only by server/ code, never
# imported by src/.
#
# TUYA_REGION names the data centre the project is bound to. This project is `sg`. A wrong
# host fails as `sign invalid`, which is indistinguishable from a bad secret; an unenabled
# one still issues a token and then refuses business calls.
#
# TUYA_ACCESS_ID=
# TUYA_ACCESS_SECRET=
# TUYA_REGION=sg

# --- Node-RED admin login (required if the flow has adminAuth) --------------
# The same login you'd use for the Node-RED editor. The repo-root .env has these too, for
# the CLI deploy scripts — but the root .env is NOT loaded by the systemd units, which use
# `EnvironmentFile=/home/bems/bems/server/.env`. Both files need them.
#
# The proxy uses them to read the live flow: `GET /api/tuya/devices` marks which vendor
# devices already have a node, and `POST /api/enroll` writes new ones.
#
# Setting them only in the root .env fails SILENTLY and was live on the Pi until
# 2026-08-25. The claimed-set read is separate from the Tuya call, so the fleet still
# returned and every device merely looked unenrolled — the enrolment wizard then offered
# all 19 already-enrolled devices as available. The endpoint now reports
# `claimed_known: false` and the wizard says so, but the fix is to set these.
#
# NODE_RED_ADMIN_USER=
# NODE_RED_ADMIN_PASS=
